{"uid":"a48b530a6d57a439","name":"test_a_cancellation_requested_from_outside_is_honoured","fullName":"tests.test_iteration_cancellation_contained#test_a_cancellation_requested_from_outside_is_honoured","historyId":"64f56ec7f18458f381f47b952a08fec1","time":{"start":1791891599275,"stop":1791891599286,"duration":11},"description":"The other half of \"deliberate\": when somebody cancels the iteration's\n    own task, the boundary must not eat the cancellation and pretend the\n    iteration merely failed.\n\n    Task 029 (#182 family, steer 023/021): `hang()` used to suspend on\n    `asyncio.sleep(3600)`, but this suite's own autouse `asyncio.sleep`\n    patch (conftest.py, \"backoff and sleep stubbed by default\") makes\n    *every* delay resolve in a single extra event-loop tick -- including\n    this one. On 3.11, `asyncio.wait_for` wraps the awaited coroutine in a\n    separate `Task` (`ensure_future` + `add_done_callback`), one scheduler\n    hop slower than 3.12+'s direct `await fut`; that one extra hop was\n    enough for the instant-patched sleep to resolve and `hang()` to return\n    *before* `task.cancel()` ran, so no CancelledError was ever raised --\n    a fixture race, not an engine defect (task 028's diagnosis). `hang()`\n    now suspends on an `asyncio.Event` that nothing in this test, this\n    module or the patched-`asyncio.sleep` machinery ever sets -- it cannot\n    resolve on its own on *any* interpreter, so the worker task is\n    provably still suspended when `task.cancel()` fires, deterministically,\n    version-independently.\n\n    Task 029, second pass: this node states BOTH halves of the contract it\n    is here to pin -- the `CancelledError` propagates out of\n    `_run_iteration_once`, *and* no `iteration.end` event is recorded for\n    the cancelled iteration. The second assertion used to live only in the\n    sibling `test_a_cancellation_mid_iteration_is_not_turned_into_a_fault`,\n    so honouring an outside cancellation and not recording it as a finished\n    iteration were pinned by two different nodes; a boundary that honoured\n    the cancel *and* emitted an end event would have passed this one. It is\n    an added assertion, not a loosened one.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>The other half of &quot;deliberate&quot;: when somebody cancels the iteration's<br />\nown task, the boundary must not eat the cancellation and pretend the<br />\niteration merely failed.</p>\n<pre><code>Task 029 (#182 family, steer 023/021): `hang()` used to suspend on\n`asyncio.sleep(3600)`, but this suite's own autouse `asyncio.sleep`\npatch (conftest.py, &quot;backoff and sleep stubbed by default&quot;) makes\n*every* delay resolve in a single extra event-loop tick -- including\nthis one. On 3.11, `asyncio.wait_for` wraps the awaited coroutine in a\nseparate `Task` (`ensure_future` + `add_done_callback`), one scheduler\nhop slower than 3.12+'s direct `await fut`; that one extra hop was\nenough for the instant-patched sleep to resolve and `hang()` to return\n*before* `task.cancel()` ran, so no CancelledError was ever raised --\na fixture race, not an engine defect (task 028's diagnosis). `hang()`\nnow suspends on an `asyncio.Event` that nothing in this test, this\nmodule or the patched-`asyncio.sleep` machinery ever sets -- it cannot\nresolve on its own on *any* interpreter, so the worker task is\nprovably still suspended when `task.cancel()` fires, deterministically,\nversion-independently.\n\nTask 029, second pass: this node states BOTH halves of the contract it\nis here to pin -- the `CancelledError` propagates out of\n`_run_iteration_once`, *and* no `iteration.end` event is recorded for\nthe cancelled iteration. The second assertion used to live only in the\nsibling `test_a_cancellation_mid_iteration_is_not_turned_into_a_fault`,\nso honouring an outside cancellation and not recording it as a finished\niteration were pinned by two different nodes; a boundary that honoured\nthe cancel *and* emitted an end event would have 